Signal renegotiation-info support with the extension, not the SCSV

RFC 5746 section 3.4 lets a client advertise renegotiation-info support on an
initial ClientHello in either of two ways: the
TLS_EMPTY_RENEGOTIATION_INFO_SCSV pseudo-cipher (0x00ff), or an empty
renegotiation_info extension (0xff01).

OpenSSL picks the SCSV. Every browser picks the extension. That one choice
costs a fingerprint match twice over: it adds a cipher suite nothing else
sends and omits an extension everything else sends, so both the cipher count
and the extension set differ from any browser.

The switch is conditioned on the minimum protocol version rather than made
unconditional, because the SCSV exists for a reason. Pre-RFC5746 servers, the
very ones that motivated it, can mishandle an unfamiliar extension. So a
client whose floor is TLS 1.2 is treated as modern and sends the extension,
while one willing to speak TLS 1.1 or older keeps the SCSV and stock
behaviour. blasthttp's compatibility path stays byte-identical to unpatched
OpenSSL; only the impersonation path changes, and it always pins its floor at
TLS 1.2.

On an initial handshake previous_client_finished_len is zero, so the existing
WPACKET_sub_memcpy_u8 call already emits the empty renegotiated_connection
field RFC 5746 requires. No new code is needed to build the body.

--- a/ssl/statem/extensions_clnt.c
+++ b/ssl/statem/extensions_clnt.c
@@ -16,8 +16,19 @@
                                           unsigned int context, X509 *x,
                                           size_t chainidx)
 {
-    /* Add RI if renegotiating */
-    if (!s->renegotiate)
+    /*
+     * Add RI if renegotiating.
+     *
+     * On an initial handshake RFC 5746 3.4 allows either this extension or
+     * the SCSV. Browsers always send the extension, so a client whose floor
+     * is TLS 1.2 does too. One willing to speak older protocols keeps the
+     * SCSV, which is safer against pre-RFC5746 servers that mishandle
+     * unfamiliar extensions. ssl_cipher_list_to_bytes() is gated on the same
+     * condition so exactly one of the two is ever sent.
+     */
+    if (!s->renegotiate
+            && (SSL_CONNECTION_IS_DTLS(s)
+                || s->min_proto_version < TLS1_2_VERSION))
         return EXT_RETURN_NOT_SENT;
 
     if (!WPACKET_put_bytes_u16(pkt, TLSEXT_TYPE_renegotiate)
--- a/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c
+++ b/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c
@@ -4063,9 +4063,14 @@
 {
     int i;
     size_t totlen = 0, len, maxlen, maxverok = 0;
+    /*
+     * Send the SCSV only when the renegotiation_info extension is not being
+     * sent. tls_construct_ctos_renegotiate() is gated on the same version
+     * test, so support is signalled exactly once, one way or the other.
+     */
     int empty_reneg_info_scsv = !s->renegotiate
                                 && (SSL_CONNECTION_IS_DTLS(s)
-                                    || s->min_proto_version < TLS1_3_VERSION);
+                                    || s->min_proto_version < TLS1_2_VERSION);
     SSL *ssl = SSL_CONNECTION_GET_SSL(s);
 
     /* Set disabled masks for this session */
